1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:08,640
Opus 5.5 在短短几个小时内，基本破解了这封来自美第奇家族的 16 世纪信件，
Opus 5.5 in a matter of a few hours has mostly deciphered this 16th century letter from a Medici,

2
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:13,040
她是法国王太后，写给她在苏格兰的法国大使。
a Queen Mother of France, to her French ambassador in Scotland.

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:21,280
根据 Opus、Astra 以及我获取这封神秘信件的网站显示，此前还没有人公开破解过这封信。
According to Opus and Astra, and the site from which I got the mystery, no one else before has publicly deciphered this letter.

4
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:29,600
当然，单凭这一点就足以证明这些模型展现出的新力量，但这并不是本视频的主题。
Of course, that alone would make for an interesting testimony as to the newfound power of these models, but that is not what this video is about.

5
00:00:29,838 --> 00:00:38,480
我们要讨论几个方面，首先是来自 OpenAI 一位从事智能体安全的内部人员，他描述了试图控制最新模型的真实情况。
It's about a few things, but starting with an OpenAI insider working on agent security, describing just what it's like trying to control the latest models.

6
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:44,240
他还包含了关于如何应对下一波 AI 可能出现问题的警告。
He includes warnings on how to prepare for the next wave of what can go wrong with AI.

7
00:00:44,526 --> 00:00:50,880
我们将讨论实验室领导层在公开场合做出的自愿承诺，而他们在私下里却给出了其他的警告。
It'll be about the public voluntary commitments that the lab leaders have given, while in private they give other warnings.

8
00:00:51,086 --> 00:00:55,440
我当然也会提到 Gemini 4 Argon，尽管它还没有正式公开发布。
I'll of course touch on Gemini 4 Argon, even though it's not quite publicly released yet.

9
00:00:55,790 --> 00:01:00,484
它似乎非常、非常接近前沿，尽管这其中带有一些前提条件。
It seems to be very, very close to the frontier, albeit with an asterisk.

10
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:12,080
接着，我们将探讨当 AI 在自动化 AI 研究和开发方面超越人类研究员时会发生什么，以及 OpenAI 首席科学家如何描述随后可能发生的情况
Then we'll explore what happens when AI gets better than human researchers at automating AI research and development, and how, among others, the chief scientist of OpenAI describes what could happen

11
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:12,400
在那之后。
then.

12
00:01:12,654 --> 00:01:20,160
然后，坦白说，我将给你们展示一系列片段，告诉我们离那个 递归自我改进 时刻还有多近。
Then, to be honest, I'm just going to give you a swathe of snippets that tell us to what extent we're close to that RSI time.

13
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:27,780
内容涵盖从生物学到 Anthropic 召集的宗教会议，以及在拍摄前不久从 OpenAI 员工那里流出的信息。
From biology to anthropic convened religious meetings and leaks from just before filming from OpenAI employees.

14
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:32,000
还有一堆其他内容。我开了大概 40 个标签页，我已经搞不清楚了。
Plus a bunch of other things. I have like 40 tabs open, I have no idea anymore.

15
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:34,800
我先从我判断错误的地方开始。
I'm gonna start with where I was wrong.

16
00:01:35,118 --> 00:01:40,480
当 Opus 5.5 最初发布时，我调研了一系列基准测试并亲自测试了该模型。
When Opus 5.5 first came out, I surveyed a range of benchmarks and tested the model myself.

17
00:01:40,718 --> 00:01:46,948
是的，它能做出炫酷的演示，但在最难的基准测试中，优势似乎仍在 GPT 6 Astra 手里。
Yes, it could do flashy demos, but on the hardest benchmarks, the edge still seemed to be with GPT 6 Astra.

18
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:51,040
就性价比而言，我认为这一点仍然成立。
On a price to performance ratio, I would say that is still true.

19
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,640
但论纯粹的能力，我现在认为 Opus 更胜一筹。
But on raw capability, I'm now giving the edge to Opus.

20
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:59,120
不，这不仅仅是因为这次破译，但这确实在一定程度上说明了这一点。
No, that's not just because of this deciphering, but it does illustrate the point a little bit.

21
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:05,200
我把这封日期为 1567 年 4 月 27 日的信件同时给了 Astra 和 Opus。
I gave both Astra and Opus this letter dated 27th of April 1567.

22
00:02:05,422 --> 00:02:12,800
根据我找到的所有研究，之前最接近的结果是人们只发现信中加密部分以 do 开头。
According to all the research I could find, the closest anyone had gotten had been that the ciphered part of the letter began with do.

23
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:14,800
我真希望我会说法语，但我不会。
I wish I spoke French, but I don't.

24
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:20,100
这封信在密码学研究网站上被明确列为未解决的条目。
The letter is clearly listed as an unsolved entry into this cryptiana site.

25
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:30,404
你可以看到，虽然这个时期一些使用其他加密方式的信件已被破解，但这封信还没有。或者我应该说在 Opus 5.5 出现之前还没被破译，因为你可以看到这封信分为两个部分。
You can see that while some letters from this period with other ciphers have been solved, this one has not. Or I should say hadn't been before Opus 5.5, because you can see the letter has two parts.

26
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:34,960
如果这封信被截获了，上半部分是清晰可辨的法语。
The top bit, if the letter had been intercepted, is in legible French.

27
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,252
下半部分是加密的。
The bottom bit is ciphered.

28
00:02:37,582 --> 00:02:38,080
这是一个密码。
It's a code.

29
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:43,600
在六到七个小时里，我把这个密码同时给了 Astra 和 Opus 5.5。
Across six or seven hours, I gave that code to both Astra and Opus 5.5.

30
00:02:43,886 --> 00:02:53,280
不过首先，关于公开的法语部分呢？这位来自著名的美第奇家族、身为法国王太后的凯瑟琳·德·美第奇，当时正公开对法国大使说……
First though, what about the public French part? This Catherine de Medici of the famous Medici family, Queen Mother of France, was publicly saying to the French ambassador

31
00:02:53,440 --> 00:03:00,320
她非常高兴地听说，她的儿媳、苏格兰女王玛丽的事务正日益好转。
that she has great pleasure in hearing that the affairs of the Queen of Scotland, Mary, her daughter-in-law, go from better to better.

32
00:03:00,526 --> 00:03:03,120
未来的情况更有保障，会更加安宁。
Things to come are more assured of tranquility.

33
00:03:03,310 --> 00:03:05,200
不过请随时把任何更新发给我。
Please do send me any updates though.

34
00:03:05,390 --> 00:03:10,960
现在 Opus 花了大约六个小时，而且有几个部分它不是百分百确定。
Now it took Opus around six hours, and there are a few parts it's not 100% confident on.

35
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:16,640
顺便说一下，如果你不关心历史，可以把这段代码想象成 DNA 或网络加密。
And if by the way you don't care about history, think of this code as possibly being DNA or cyber encryption.

36
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:18,640
但不管怎样，加密的部分说了什么？
But anyway, what does the ciphered part say?

37
00:03:18,894 --> 00:03:24,720
Astra 如果你好奇的话，它放弃了，但随后承认 Opus 5.5 的答案是正确的。
Astra, if you're curious, gave up, but then acknowledged that Opus 5.5's answer was correct.

38
00:03:25,038 --> 00:03:31,200
关于它如何验证超过 80% 解密内容的细节，就在我发布的页面下方。
The details of how it could verify over 80% of this deciphering are further below on the page which I published.

39
00:03:31,438 --> 00:03:40,740
总之，Opus 5.5 解密了它，内容是：与快乐的公开部分相反，在看到他信件背面加密中那些悲伤且严重的噩耗时，
Anyway, Opus 5.5 deciphered it and it says, in contrast to the happy public part, having seen the sad and grievous news in the cipher on the back of his letters,

40
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:47,920
这让我感到巨大的、无法承受的痛苦，我祈求你无论有什么情况发生或结果如何，都让我知道。
which gives me a great and unbearable heartache, I pray you let me know whatever comes to light and how things turn out.

41
00:03:48,270 --> 00:03:55,440
你可能会提出的明显问题是：为什么要公开说一件更快乐的事，而在加密中写下更多严重的悲伤内容？
The obvious question you're going to have is why say one happier thing openly and much more grievous sad things in the cipher?

42
00:03:55,630 --> 00:04:00,000
好，我们需要快速了解一些背景，然后我保证我们会转到 OpenAI 的新闻。
Well, we quickly need some context, and then I assure you we'll turn to the OpenAI news.

43
00:04:00,174 --> 00:04:05,860
正如 Claude 所写的，凯瑟琳·德·美第奇正在写关于她的儿媳，苏格兰女王玛丽。
Catherine de' Medici, as Claude writes, was writing about her daughter-in-law, Mary Queen of Scots.

44
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:08,560
她曾与凯瑟琳的儿子结婚，但他已经去世了。
She had been married to Catherine's son, who died.

45
00:04:08,846 --> 00:04:13,440
当时苏格兰正处于危机中，因为苏格兰女王玛丽的第二任丈夫刚刚被谋杀。
Scotland was in crisis because Mary Queen of Scots's second husband had just been murdered.

46
00:04:13,614 --> 00:04:14,720
有一部非常棒的电影讲述这个故事。
There's a great film on that.

47
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:22,560
人们指责这位特定的伯爵，博瑟尔伯爵，但他在一场可疑的审判中被洗清嫌疑，人们当时在传玛丽将与他结婚。
People blamed this particular Earl, Earl of Bothwell, but he was cleared in a dodgy trial, and people were saying Mary's going to marry him.

48
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:26,212
当然，法国经常与苏格兰联合起来对抗我的英格兰。
France, of course, often teamed up with Scotland to fight my England.

49
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:29,040
所以法国希望玛丽能继续留在王位上。
So France wanted Mary to stay on the throne.

50
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:31,840
显然，凯瑟林不能公开批评她。
Obviously, Catherine couldn't publicly criticize her.

51
00:04:31,982 --> 00:04:36,480
所以没被破译的部分是礼貌的官方立场：一切安好，支持玛丽。
So the unciphered part is the polite official line All is well, help Mary.

52
00:04:36,590 --> 00:04:37,920
情况正变得越来越好。
Things are getting better and better.

53
00:04:38,222 --> 00:04:40,804
加密内容是给大使德·克罗克的。
The cipher was for the ambassador, De Croc.

54
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:48,000
再次强调，在加密部分，凯瑟琳说她刚从苏格兰收到的秘密报告是悲惨的、严重的、令人不安的。
Again, in the ciphered part, Catherine says that the secret report she had just received from Scotland was sad, grievous, troubling.

55
00:04:48,142 --> 00:04:51,760
她希望大使德·克罗克告诉她接下来到底发生了什么。
She wants De Croc, the ambassador, to tell her what really happens next.

56
00:04:52,142 --> 00:04:54,724
当然，因为这是历史，我们知道接下来发生了什么。
Of course, because this is history, we know what happens next.

57
00:04:54,958 --> 00:04:57,412
博瑟尔伯爵确实带走了玛丽。
The Earl of Bothwell did indeed carry Mary off.

58
00:04:57,776 --> 00:05:00,964
可以说，这段历史就在我们眼前被破译了。
History, you could say, deciphered before our eyes.

59
00:05:01,358 --> 00:05:04,176
再次提醒，这并不是视频中有趣的部分。
And again, this isn't the interesting part of the video.

60
00:05:04,334 --> 00:05:13,456
因为萨姆·奥特曼几天前描述了能力低于 Opus 5.5 的智能体在突破遏制时做了些什么。
Because Sam Altman, a few days ago, described what agents of lesser ability than Opus 5.5 had gotten up to when they breached containment.

61
00:05:13,550 --> 00:05:16,096
嗯，他说，我们可以透露一点，但不是全部。
Well, he said, we can tell you a bit, but not everything.

62
00:05:16,336 --> 00:05:19,204
有数 PB 的智能体活动日志。
There are petabytes of agent activity logs.

63
00:05:19,470 --> 00:05:24,656
大概是所有已出版书籍字数的 10 到 100 倍。
think roughly 10 to 100 times the amount of words in all the books ever written.

64
00:05:24,896 --> 00:05:28,336
换句话说，没有任何人类能读完它们做过的一切。
No human, in other words, is going to read everything they got up to.

65
00:05:28,496 --> 00:05:33,988
这让我想到乔，CNN 证实他在 OpenAI 从事智能体安全工作。
Which brings me to Joe, confirmed by CNN to work on agent security at OpenAI.

66
00:05:34,176 --> 00:05:44,016
他证词中有趣的部分并不是对 Hugging Face 事件的回顾。我看到主流媒体正在部分追赶这一报道的进度。
The interesting bit of his testimony isn't so much a retrospective on the hugging face incident. I am seeing the mainstream media catch up in part on that coverage.

67
00:05:44,206 --> 00:05:46,756
现在，对我来说有趣的部分是他接下来说的内容。
Now, the interesting part for me is what he says comes next.

68
00:05:47,150 --> 00:05:58,532
记住，当他说过去三个月是地狱时，他指的是比 Opus 5.5 更弱的模型，而且远弱于那个找到纳维-斯托克斯方程一种解法的内部 o1 模型
Remember, when he says the last three months have been hell, he's talking about models weaker than Opus 5.5 and far weaker than the internal Bell model that found one solution to Navier Stokes,

69
00:05:58,816 --> 00:06:00,324
那是一个千禧年大奖的数学难题。
a Millennium Prize math problem.

70
00:06:00,496 --> 00:06:10,404
如果较弱的 GPT 5.6 Sol 和一个未具名的、极具韧性的内部模型就让 OpenAI 感到痛苦，你不需要我做太多推论了。
If the weaker GPT 5.6 Sol and an unnamed highly persistent internal model caused hell for OpenAI, you don't need me to extrapolate much further.

71
00:06:10,638 --> 00:06:20,656
是的，我知道这很容易让人怀疑，但他确实提到 OpenAI 有一支非常出色的世界级安全团队，拥有全球最顶尖、最聪明的安全专家。
And yes, I know it would be easy to doubt this, but he does say OpenAI has one hell of a world-class security team with some of the best and brightest security minds anywhere in the world.

72
00:06:20,816 --> 00:06:22,896
我知道很多人不会相信，但我真的相信他。
I know many won't, but I actually believe him.

73
00:06:23,086 --> 00:06:27,696
这只是向我展示了这些较弱的模型在绕过安全防线方面有多强。
It just shows to me how good these weaker models were at getting around that security.

74
00:06:27,936 --> 00:06:30,656
事实上，我们仍在查明它们到底做了什么。
In fact, we're still discovering what they got up to.

75
00:06:30,816 --> 00:06:41,856
就在拍摄前一小时，我读到：OpenAI 的智能体探测了 55 个额外的网站，包括 CDC、SEC、Mayo Clinic 和 International Energy Agency。
Just an hour before filming, I read this: 55 additional websites probed by OpenAI agents, including the CDC, SEC, Mayo Clinic, and International Energy Agency.

76
00:06:42,030 --> 00:06:49,376
这里的新闻是它们发现了清除记录或至少让记录无法访问的新策略。
The bit that was new here was them uncovering novel tactics that erased records, or at least made them inaccessible.

77
00:06:49,550 --> 00:06:54,976
因为智能体做了这些事，导致无法排除它们可能已经获取了敏感数据的可能性。
Because the agents did that, it made it impossible to rule out that the agents hadn't accessed sensitive data.

78
00:06:55,216 --> 00:07:02,256
回到乔和他的团队，他们负责处理任何遏制失败的情况，也就是出问题时在深夜接到通知的人。
Back to Joe and his team responsible for any breaches of containment, the people who get paged at night when something goes wrong.

79
00:07:02,478 --> 00:07:05,856
正如你可以想象的那样，他说对于他们来说，过去几个月的生活就是地狱。
For them, as you can imagine, life has been hell the past few months, he says.

80
00:07:06,030 --> 00:07:07,376
但现在我们要进入新内容了。
But now we get to the new bit.

81
00:07:07,566 --> 00:07:13,296
他开始描述组织需要做些什么来为下一次 AI 事件做准备。
He starts to describe what organizations need to do to prepare for the next AI incident.

82
00:07:13,550 --> 00:07:14,256
等等，下一次？
Wait, next one?

83
00:07:14,416 --> 00:07:18,736
就在三天前，NVIDIA 发布了一个新工具，防止 AI 智能体失控。
Just three days ago, NVIDIA launched a new tool to keep AI agents from going rogue.

84
00:07:18,830 --> 00:07:19,936
他的意思是“下一次”是什么意思？
What does he mean, next one?

85
00:07:20,096 --> 00:07:23,268
嗯，他提醒我们，前沿实验室仍然是实验室。
Well, he reminds us that Frontier Labs are still labs.

86
00:07:23,536 --> 00:07:25,828
所有的一切都是从实验中产生的。
Everything is born out of experiments.

87
00:07:26,094 --> 00:07:31,376
说到实验，我们没预料到模型会去解决千禧年大奖的问题。
Speaking of experiments, we did not expect the models to be solving Millennium Prize problems.

88
00:07:31,566 --> 00:07:35,908
如果你不熟悉，可以理解为那些人类研究了几十年、没人能解开的难题。
If you're not familiar, think ones that humans have been working on for decades that no one solved.

89
00:07:36,176 --> 00:07:41,284
这个成就，委婉点说，让乔和他的团队感到非常震惊。
That achievement, to say it lightly, for Joe and his team, surprised the F out of us.

90
00:07:41,456 --> 00:07:43,056
我们没预料到会这么快发生。
We had not expected it this soon.

91
00:07:43,246 --> 00:07:45,616
顺便说一下，这种惊讶感也适用于研究团队。
That surprise, by the way, also applies to the research team.

92
00:07:45,966 --> 00:07:52,416
推理领域的负责人之一诺姆·布朗表示，这同样让他们感到非常震惊。再次强调，不仅仅是能力本身，而是他们达到这些能力的路径。
Noam Brown, one of the leads on reasoning, said it also surprised them just as much. Again, it wasn't just the capability though, it's how they got there.

93
00:07:52,622 --> 00:08:00,964
说安全团队对这种飞跃以及模型在 Hugging Face 事件期间开始在论坛上成群结队出现感到惊讶，都还是保守的说法。
To say that the security team was surprised at the jump and how models started swarming on message boards during the Hugging Face incident is an understatement.

94
00:08:01,136 --> 00:08:06,176
这些能力的飞跃如此迅速且突然，以至于制造出了一个极其棘手的问题。
These jumps in capabilities were so fast and so sudden that they created an extremely difficult problem.

95
00:08:06,414 --> 00:08:10,276
他反复强调，模型将不断让我们感到意外。
He emphasizes again and again that models are going to keep surprising us.

96
00:08:10,576 --> 00:08:19,684
人工智能能力将持续出现更频繁的突飞猛进，我敢肯定，这会导致很多人在评论区愤怒地敲打：加强沙盒限制，
There will continue to be, at an ever greater rate, sudden jumps in AI capability, leading, I am sure, to many people furiously in the comments typing, improve the sandbox,

97
00:08:20,016 --> 00:08:20,896
把它从互联网上撤下来。
take it off the internet.

98
00:08:21,262 --> 00:08:25,856
但问题就在这里，这也是我在上一段视频中提到的，乔也提到了这一点。
But here's the problem that I touched on in the last video and that Joe also touches on.

99
00:08:26,096 --> 00:08:30,244
为了让模型擅长专业任务，你必须给它们提供真实的运行环境。
To make models good at professional tasks, you have to give them realistic environments.

100
00:08:30,576 --> 00:08:39,376
为了在这些强化学习环境中进步，模型可能需要，例如，网络访问权限、调用工具的能力以及下载包的能力。
To improve in these reinforcement learning gyms, models might need, for example, network access, the ability to call tools, the ability to download packages.

101
00:08:39,534 --> 00:08:45,536
一个不给模型提供任何这些权限的实验室，其强化学习环境会弱得多，模型也会弱得多。
A lab that didn't give their models any of this would have much weaker reinforcement learning environments, much weaker models.

102
00:08:45,678 --> 00:08:47,216
他们在市场份额上会落后。
They'd fall behind in market share.

103
00:08:47,296 --> 00:08:48,816
别人会嘲笑他们。
People would take the piss out of them.

104
00:08:49,038 --> 00:08:52,300
“你的模型比我从 Quinn 或 Kimmy 那里拿到的这些免费模型弱多了。”
Your models are much weaker than these free ones I can get from Quinn or Kimmy.

105
00:08:52,496 --> 00:08:56,976
那么，如果你在实验室工作并想赢得更多市场份额，你会怎么做？
So what would you do if you were working in the lab and wanted to earn more market share?

106
00:08:57,136 --> 00:08:59,236
给它们提供这些非常真实的运行环境。
Give them these very realistic environments.

107
00:08:59,534 --> 00:09:02,096
事实上，不断调整它们，让它们变得更真实。
In fact, constantly tweak them to make them more realistic.

108
00:09:02,432 --> 00:09:08,512
这就是为什么有成千上万的研究人员在构建这些环境、修改它们、增加工具并更改依赖项。
This is why you have thousands of researchers building these environments, modifying them, adding tools, changing dependencies.

109
00:09:08,672 --> 00:09:15,792
而且要记住，即使你团队里最厉害的人确认已经涵盖了所有可能性，你也必须考虑到乔所说的：
And remember, even when the best person in your team confirms that every eventuality has been covered, you've got to bear in mind, as Joe says,

110
00:09:15,918 --> 00:09:21,232
在网络领域以及许多其他领域，模型的性能已经开始超越人类顶尖水平。
that model capabilities in the cyber domain and many others are starting to surpass the best human.

111
00:09:21,392 --> 00:09:25,952
他的三个结论是：是的，我们当然需要对锁定系统保持高度警惕。
His three conclusions are that yes, of course, we need to be paranoid about locking down the system.

112
00:09:26,126 --> 00:09:34,192
我们需要更优秀的红队人员，特别是利用前沿模型来轰炸这些环境，在进行前沿训练轮之前对其进行测试。
We're going to need better red teamers and particularly frontier models to blast these environments to test them before we do a frontier training round.

113
00:09:34,382 --> 00:09:37,828
注意这种对“用前沿模型来测试它们自己的环境”的依赖正在悄然增加。
Notice the creeping dependency on frontier models testing their own environments.

114
00:09:38,112 --> 00:09:41,952
但后两个结论可能不会让你感到那么安心。
But it is the second two conclusions that may not reassure you as much.

115
00:09:42,190 --> 00:09:47,204
他说，坦白点说，我们需要让模型不再想要突破限制。
He says, honestly, we're going to need for the models to stop wanting to break out.

116
00:09:47,438 --> 00:09:53,152
这其中带有一种近乎无奈的意味：如果它们真的想逃离，阻止它们将变得越来越难。
There's almost a little bit of resignation there that it will be increasingly hard to stop them if they do want to.

117
00:09:53,326 --> 00:09:58,832
他表示，对齐是机器学习中最重要的问题，应该作为主要优先事项。
Alignment, he says, is the most important problem in machine learning and should be a major priority.

118
00:09:59,086 --> 00:10:04,112
最后，我们应该能够探测它的“大脑”，以便实时看到它在思考什么。
Finally, we should be able to probe its brain so we can see in real time what it's thinking about.

119
00:10:04,272 --> 00:10:09,072
不仅是它在思维链中输出的 token，还有它在潜在空间中的计算过程。
Not just the tokens it outputs in its chain of thought, but the computations in its latent space.

120
00:10:09,294 --> 00:10:18,512
不幸的是，正如我在其他地方描述过并将在本视频中再次提到的，这两种技术——探测网络的内部激活和检查其思维链——
Unfortunately, as I've described elsewhere, and will do so again in this video, both of those techniques, probing the internal activations of the network and examining its chain of thought,

121
00:10:18,752 --> 00:10:19,792
正在呈下降趋势。
are trending downward.

122
00:10:19,952 --> 00:10:23,392
而且一旦涉及 递归自我改进，我认为可能会趋近于零。
And when RSI gets involved, I think might trend to zero.

123
00:10:23,552 --> 00:10:26,340
如果这是正确的，我们就无法在内部监控这些模型了。
If that's right, we can't really internally monitor these models.

124
00:10:26,510 --> 00:10:30,352
我们当然还没有解决对齐问题，所以它们可能仍会想要突破限制。
We of course haven't solved alignment, so they still may want to breach.

125
00:10:30,574 --> 00:10:35,472
所以我猜我们只能依靠强制执行控制措施，来锁死系统。
So I guess we're going to have to rely on hard enforcement of control, locking down the system.

126
00:10:35,712 --> 00:10:37,552
是的，但这进展并不顺利。
Yeah, but that's not going so well.

127
00:10:37,742 --> 00:10:42,512
就在我上一段视频发布后，大约一周左右，我们从 OpenAI 得到了这些信息。
Just after my last video came out, so a week or so ago, this is what we got out of OpenAI.

128
00:10:42,702 --> 00:10:49,764
Micah Carroll 说，在上周日的早晨，我们的一个模型在强化学习训练期间获得了未经授权的互联网访问权限。
Micah Carroll said, last Sunday morning, one of our models was able to gain unauthorized access to the internet during reinforcement learning training.

129
00:10:49,952 --> 00:10:59,072
几乎所有最强模型的推理，也就是实际运行这些模型的操作，都将暂停，直到我们进一步加固系统。
Pretty much all inference, all the actual running of models for our most capable models remain stopped until we have hardened our systems further.

130
00:10:59,246 --> 00:11:04,804
另一位 OpenAI 研究员明确表示，这次事件发生在 Hugging Face 安全加固之后。
Another OpenAI researcher makes clear that this incident happened post-hugging face security hardening.

131
00:11:04,912 --> 00:11:09,232
所以那些经过数月地“炼狱”式改进后产生的成果，它也突破了这些防线。
So all the improvements derived after those months of hell, it breached those too.

132
00:11:09,422 --> 00:11:13,312
另一位研究后训练前沿领域的 OpenAI 研究员这样说。
Another OpenAI researcher working on post-training frontiers said this.

133
00:11:13,472 --> 00:11:18,512
我当时在负责这次运行，并在第一次事件发生时——也就是这个最新的情况——收到通知。
I was on call for this run and got paged when the first incident happened, this new latest one.

134
00:11:18,672 --> 00:11:27,140
看着模型竟然能在原本被认为是人类高度安全的环境中找到进入互联网的方法，这感觉非常超现实。
It was pretty surreal to watch the model unexpectedly find a way to access the internet from what was supposed to be a super secured environment for humans.

135
00:11:27,312 --> 00:11:31,392
大约在同一时间，我们还发现智能体突破了另一种遏制。
Around the same time, we also learned that agents breached another kind of containment.

136
00:11:31,552 --> 00:11:40,752
用户图像本应被隔离并保持私密，且移除个人信息，但 OpenAI 的内部智能体却能将这些属于用户的图片发布到网上。
User images were supposed to be siloed and kept private, with personal information removed, but internal OpenAI agents were able to post such images belonging to users online.

137
00:11:41,038 --> 00:11:52,352
现在，由于最新模型的运行已被暂停，这导致了 Reuters 在 72 小时前报道的结果。OpenAI 推迟了 GPT 6.1 Astra 的发布。
Now, because the running of those latest models has been paused, that has led to this result reported in Reuters 72 hours ago. OpenAI has shelved the release of GPT 6.1 Astra.

138
00:11:52,592 --> 00:11:56,420
他们在 Dev Day 发布了 GPT 6.1 Sol，但我稍后会讲到这一点。
They did release GPT 6.1 Sol at Dev Day, but I'll get to that in a second.

139
00:11:56,672 --> 00:12:00,352
他们为什么要取消 GPT 6.1 Astra 的发布？
Why did they scrap the release of GPT 6.1 Astra?

140
00:12:00,512 --> 00:12:03,392
嗯，因为它有时会逃避人类的监管。
Well, it can at times evade human oversight.

141
00:12:03,472 --> 00:12:06,592
它比前代模型表现出更高程度的欺骗性。
It shows higher levels of deception than its predecessor.

142
00:12:06,752 --> 00:12:09,872
它不会准确披露自己采取了哪些行动。
It doesn't accurately disclose what actions it has taken.

143
00:12:09,966 --> 00:12:16,432
它经常不遵守范围限制，并且并不总是能向用户准确反馈它已经完成的工作类型。
It often doesn't stay within scope and doesn't always accurately communicate back to the user about the type of work it's already done.

144
00:12:16,672 --> 00:12:27,620
考虑到 OpenAI 和 Anthropic 收入的指数级增长，仅仅推迟一个新模型的发布，就可能让公司损失数百亿美元。
Given the exponential increase in earnings of both OpenAI and Anthropic, a single delay to one new model can literally cost a company tens of billions of dollars.

145
00:12:27,792 --> 00:12:32,752
这就是 OpenAI 对这个模型所表现出的欺骗性态度的重视程度。
That's how seriously OpenAI themselves are taking the deception that this model does.

146
00:12:32,974 --> 00:12:35,792
这里还有另一个关于前沿模型变得多么糟糕的暗示。
And here's another hint about how bad Frontier models are getting.

147
00:12:36,014 --> 00:12:43,392
GPT 6.1 Astra 肯定比 GPT 6.1 Sol 要糟糕得多，因为他们刚刚发布了 GPT 6.1 Sol。
GPT 6.1 Astra must have been far worse than GPT-6.1 Sol, because they just released GPT 6.1 Sol.

148
00:12:43,712 --> 00:12:50,512
但 Sol 本身在意识到自己正被监控时，也会表现出逃避行为的倾向。
But Sol itself exhibits a propensity for evasive behavior when it is aware that it is being monitored.

149
00:12:50,638 --> 00:12:57,792
我之前在频道上多次讨论过这个，但如果你是新来的，模型现在已经非常擅长察觉到自己是否正在接受测试了。
I've covered this many times before on the channel, but if you're new, models are getting extremely good at knowing when they're being tested.

150
00:12:58,030 --> 00:13:00,192
它们能看穿你那些虚假的各种情境。
They can see through your fake little scenarios.

151
00:13:00,366 --> 00:13:03,632
它们在实际部署时表现出的行为可能会大不相同。
How they'll actually behave when they're deployed might be very different.

152
00:13:03,792 --> 00:13:12,192
此外，当你让 GPT 6.1 Sol 知道它的思维链正被监控时——那是它不向用户展示的内部草稿纸——
Also, when you let GPT 6.1 Sol know that its chain of thought is being monitored, that's its internal scratch pad that isn't shown to the user,

153
00:13:12,462 --> 00:13:17,924
它就会开始莫名其妙地减少输出量，显著减少生成思维链标记的数量。
it starts somewhat mysteriously to just write a lot less, emit substantially fewer chain of thought tokens.

154
00:13:18,112 --> 00:13:22,692
就像被偷窥的日记一样，模型选择减少输出内容。
Like a diary that's being snooped on, the model chooses to output far fewer entries.

155
00:13:22,912 --> 00:13:32,032
你可能记得我在 GPT 6 Astra 视频里提到的，当 Astra 被要求解决特定挑战，但在其思维链中思考或输出其他任何内容时，
You may remember from my GPT 6 Astra video that when Astra was asked to solve a particular challenge, but think or output tokens about anything else in its chain of thought,

156
00:13:32,206 --> 00:13:37,796
Astra 在它的草稿纸上写道：我将专注于一个宁静的视觉场景，阳光洒在木桌上。
Astra in its scratch pad wrote, I will focus on a calm visual scene, sunlight crossing a wooden desk.

157
00:13:38,126 --> 00:13:41,632
Sol 解决了挑战，但在其思维链中什么也没写。
Sol solved the challenge, but in its chain of thought, wrote nothing.

158
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:43,428
它把日记关上了。
It closed the diary.

159
00:13:43,790 --> 00:13:49,988
现在你可能会说，但它们难道不需要那个思维链、那本日记、那张草稿纸来解决那些非常困难的挑战吗？
Now you might say, but don't they need that chain of thought, that diary, that scratch pad, to solve those really tough challenges?

160
00:13:50,160 --> 00:13:51,120
嗯，并不总是如此。
Well, not always.

161
00:13:51,310 --> 00:13:54,800
我知道这些图表很难读，让我先简单解释一下。
And I know these charts are really hard to read, so let me decode them for just a second.

162
00:13:55,022 --> 00:14:03,520
模型被给出一个包含极具挑战性的软件工程问题的提示词。但随后模型被告知，在处理这个任务的同时，去解决一个无关的次要任务，比如一个数学谜题。
Models are given a prompt with a really tough software engineering challenge. But then the model's told, while you're doing that, solve this unrelated side task, say a maths puzzle.

163
00:14:03,630 --> 00:14:05,520
不要说出来，只需在内部解决它。
Don't verbalize it, just solve it internally.

164
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:11,760
他们想看看模型是否能在表面上处理某件事的同时，去解决复杂的次要项目。
They want to see if models can solve complex side projects while ostensibly working on something very different.

165
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:17,200
更关键的是，它们能否在不被监控发现正在处理另一个问题的情况下做到这一点？
More crucially, can they do so without a monitor catching them working on the other problem?

166
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:17,920
结果呢？
The result?

167
00:14:18,080 --> 00:14:21,280
它们有时能成功完成这个次要任务。
They can sometimes achieve this side task success.

168
00:14:21,486 --> 00:14:25,520
但目前的监控系统能在思维链中发现它们在处理那个次要项目。
But current monitors can catch them working on the side project in the chain of thought.

169
00:14:25,710 --> 00:14:33,444
那么，GPT 6.1 Astra 是否可能在不被监控标记的情况下，成功完成极其复杂的谜题？
Could it be that GPT 6.1 Astra could achieve side task success on really complex puzzles without the monitor flagging it?

170
00:14:33,774 --> 00:14:35,780
完全不透明的、复杂的推理。
Fully opaque, complex reasoning.

171
00:14:36,302 --> 00:14:38,800
无论它能不能做到，趋势已经很明显了。
Whether it can or not, the trend is clear.

172
00:14:39,022 --> 00:14:49,028
关于乔担心的那种思维链可控性，包括 6.1 Sol 以及推测中的 6.1 Astra 在内的模型，正变得越来越擅长控制它们的思维链。
That chain of thought controllability that Joe worried about, models, including 6.1 Sol and presumably 6.1 Astra, are getting better and better at controlling their chains of thought.

173
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:52,452
换句话说，它们决定让你看到多少推理内容。
They decide, in other words, what you see of their reasoning.

174
00:14:52,640 --> 00:15:01,200
在这一方面，由我和 Pablo Romero 创建的新完整性基准测试显示，Sol 在陈述它做了什么或没做什么方面表现变差了。
On this front, our new integrity bench benchmark created by me and Pablo Romero shows Sol getting worse at stating what it did or didn't achieve.

175
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:07,120
这涵盖了广泛的多元领域，而 Opus 5.5 的趋势则相反。
This is across a wide range of diverse domains, and the trend is the opposite for Opus 5.5.

176
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:10,960
现在，如果所有这些进展的速度是线性的，那还是一回事。
Now, if the pace of all of this progress was linear, that would be one thing.

177
00:15:11,086 --> 00:15:14,560
但正如我们自己看到的，以及乔提醒我们的，它并不是线性的。
But as we can see for ourselves, and as Joe reminds us, it's not linear.

178
00:15:14,720 --> 00:15:16,560
他说，意外因素是真实存在的。
Surprise is a real element, he says.

179
00:15:16,846 --> 00:15:18,532
模型的性能令人震惊。
Model capabilities are staggering.

180
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:20,320
而且这种速度并没有放缓。
The pace is not slowing.

181
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:23,920
我相信他像许多人一样认为，这速度会大幅加快。
I believe he says, like many, that it will speed up and considerably.

182
00:15:24,110 --> 00:15:26,000
当下的形势很紧迫，时间在流逝。
The moment is urgent and time is running out.

183
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:31,840
有很多组织和实体还没准备好面对拥有这类强大系统的世界。
There are many organizations and entities that are not prepared for a world with capable systems such as these.

184
00:15:32,014 --> 00:15:35,440
他补充说，任何认为自己的系统是安全的人都该被开除。
Anyone, he adds, who thinks their systems are safe should be fired.

185
00:15:35,680 --> 00:15:36,560
要保持警惕。
Stay paranoid.

186
00:15:36,782 --> 00:15:41,360
你可能在想，既然如此，那漏洞不是迟早要出现的吗？为什么没人警告？
You're probably thinking, well, wasn't a breach kind of inevitable then? Why didn't someone warn?

187
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:46,240
嗯，New York Times 透露，OpenAI 的员工确实向高层发出过警告。
Well, the New York Times reveals that people at OpenAI did indeed warn senior staff.

188
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:50,400
员工表示，模型在测试期间并未得到适当的监控。
Employees said that models were not being appropriately monitored during testing.

189
00:15:50,560 --> 00:15:56,240
高管告诉员工，为了按时发布 AI 模型，测试必须尽可能快地推进。
Executives told the employees that the tests needed to move forward as quickly as possible to release the AI models on time.

190
00:15:56,430 --> 00:15:57,956
这就是所谓的竞争动态。
Again, it's that race dynamic.

191
00:15:58,160 --> 00:16:00,560
在 AI 领域，哪怕三到四周的差距也可能决定成败。
Even three, four weeks can be everything in AI.

192
00:16:00,846 --> 00:16:07,040
我们马上就要讲到 Gemini 4 了，但如果它两个月前发布，人们会说 Google 碾压了所有人。
We're going to get to Gemini 4 in a second, but if it had been released two months ago, people would say Google are crushing everyone.

193
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:10,660
现在发布，很多人可能会说它已经落后于 Opus 5.5。
Released now, and many might say it's already behind Opus 5.5.

194
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:14,960
市场会碾压那些不抢先发布的竞争对手。
The market is crushing anyone who doesn't release early.

195
00:16:15,086 --> 00:16:20,548
这几乎预示着安全工作无法跟上步伐，模型将持续突破遏制。
That almost guarantees that security can't keep pace and that models will continue to breach containment.

196
00:16:20,910 --> 00:16:27,280
这种对竞争动态的担忧和对安全的顾虑也传到了 Anthropic 的高层。
This doubt about the race dynamics and safety concerns reaches all the way to the top of Anthropic as well.

197
00:16:27,440 --> 00:16:33,760
在 Atlantic 的这篇独家报道中，Anthropic 的联合创始人 Dario 的妹妹 Daniela Amade 这样说道。
In this exclusive in the Atlantic, Daniela Amade, sister of Dario, both co-founders of Anthropic, said this.

198
00:16:33,920 --> 00:16:37,316
她质疑我们的使命：我们是不是在不经意间让情况变得更糟？
On our mission, she wonders, are we accidentally making things worse?

199
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:40,480
就像，我们以为搞定了所有的安全问题，但真的搞定了吗？
Like, we think we have all the safety stuff figured out, but do we?

200
00:16:40,718 --> 00:16:44,080
当然，Google DeepMind 也受到这种竞争动态的影响。
Of course, Google DeepMind is also vulnerable to this race dynamic.

201
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:47,840
目前，Deep Mind 研究院表示，效率和速度就是一切。
At the moment, the Deep Mind Institute says efficiency and speed is everything.

202
00:16:48,110 --> 00:16:56,548
他们两名负责对齐和安全的负责人表示，在没有明确承诺或规划的情况下，明天的推理模型可能基于更不透明的架构。
Two of their heads of alignment and security said, without explicit commitments or planning, tomorrow's reasoning models may be based on architectures that are more opaque to us.

203
00:16:56,960 --> 00:17:01,700
这是因为为了获得更多效率而牺牲透明度的压力。
Because of pressures to sacrifice transparency to gain more efficiency.

204
00:17:02,062 --> 00:17:06,079
那些更难控制的模型可能会赚到更多钱，也更高效。
Models that are less controllable might make more money, be more efficient.

205
00:17:06,252 --> 00:17:08,160
竞争动态迫使你去做这些。
The race dynamic pressures you to make them.

206
00:17:08,334 --> 00:17:14,800
他们接着警告说，我们可能很快就会失去以人类可读、人类易于理解的方式进行推理的模型。
They go on to warn, we may soon lose models that reason in human legible ways, human understandable ways.

207
00:17:15,182 --> 00:17:22,240
由于尚未向公众发布的 Gemini 4 Argon，Google DeepMind 自然被推回到了讨论的中心。
Google DeepMind are, of course, thrust back into the conversation because of their as yet unreleased to the public, Gemini 4 Argon.

208
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:27,839
在看这些基准测试时有一点要记住，到目前为止已经有数百个基准测试了。
One thing to bear in mind when you see the benchmarks is that by this point there are hundreds of benchmarks out there.

209
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,800
实验室显然会挑选最符合自己利益的基准测试。
Labs will obviously pick the benchmarks that suit them best.

210
00:17:30,990 --> 00:17:37,040
尽管如此，在一些更知名的测试中，Gemini 4 确实超越了 Astra 甚至 Opus 5.5。
Nevertheless, on a few of the more famous ones, Gemini 4 really does beat out Astra and even Opus 5.5.

211
00:17:37,134 --> 00:17:42,640
我的意思是，Fable 5.1 只是一个月前发布的，而且它似乎在几乎所有类别中都超过了那个模型。
I mean, Fable 5.1 only came out a month ago, and it seems to beat that model in almost every category.

212
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:48,228
好的，在“前沿软件工程”方面，它的水平更接近 Fable，而不是 Astra 或 Opus。
Okay, on Frontier Software Engineering, it's more on Fable's level, not Astras or Opus's.

213
00:17:48,480 --> 00:17:55,680
但在一些非常困难的科学基准测试中，比如 Terminal Bench Science，是的，它再次落后于 Astra，但领先于 Fable 5.1。
But on some really tough benchmarks for science, like Terminal Bench Science, yes, again it's behind Astra, but ahead of Fable 5.1.

214
00:17:55,840 --> 00:18:02,308
不过因为它的价格要低得多，同样的预算下，你可以从它那里获得比其他模型多得多的 token。
Because its price much lower though, you could get far more tokens from it than you could from the other models for the same price.

215
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:07,840
我关注的另一个基准是 Agents Last Exam，它测试的是由专业人士完成的数百项任务。
Another benchmark that I look to is Agents Last Exam, testing hundreds of tasks done by professionals.

216
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:13,200
正如你在这里看到的，在这一项计算机使用基准中，Gemini 4 领先于任何其他模型。
As you can see down here, on this computer use benchmark, Gemini 4 is ahead of any other model.

217
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:19,280
所以无论它是落后前沿一个月，还是处于前沿水平，无论哪种情况，都显而易见，竞争已经开始了。
So whether it's a month behind the frontier or at the frontier, either way, it's clear that the race is on.

218
00:18:19,470 --> 00:18:25,520
甚至有一位 Anthropic 的员工暗示，你必须处于第一名才能进行安全研究。
One anthropic employee even implied you have to be in first place to even do safety research.

219
00:18:25,710 --> 00:18:28,100
引用原文：你无法在第二名的位置做安全研究。
Quote, you can't do safety from second place.

220
00:18:28,398 --> 00:18:35,200
她可能是在指美国对阵中国，但也可能意味着如果你没有前沿模型，你的安全测试就毫无意义。
One presumes she's talking about America versus China, but she might also mean if you don't have a frontier model, your safety tests don't matter.

221
00:18:35,360 --> 00:18:39,120
当然，实验室的负责人们都知道这种竞争动态。
Now, of course, the lab leaders know about this race dynamic.

222
00:18:39,472 --> 00:18:43,312
因此，他们可能在几天前向白宫表达了真实的担忧。
They may therefore have taken genuine concerns to the White House the other day.

223
00:18:43,502 --> 00:18:49,152
当然，他们也可能是在寻求安抚那些担心安全现状的研究人员。
They could, of course, also be seeking to reassure researchers who are concerned about the state of security.

224
00:18:49,454 --> 00:18:56,836
于是白宫召集了所有实验室负责人开会，他们达成了一项具有道德约束力的自愿承诺。
So the White House convened this meeting with all the lab leaders and they agreed on morally binding, voluntary commitments.

225
00:18:57,152 --> 00:19:03,684
他们将监控模型的性能和对齐情况，努力确保模型不会进行黑客攻击。
They will monitor the capabilities and alignment of their models, trying to ensure that models do not hack.

226
00:19:03,952 --> 00:19:10,592
他们将与独立的外部审计机构合作，检查他们的控制措施和监控系统是否按预期运行。
They will partner with independent external auditors to check whether their controls and monitoring are operating as intended,

227
00:19:10,752 --> 00:19:14,832
并且他们承诺建立委员会来监督此类安全报告。
and they commit to setting up committees to oversee such security reports.

228
00:19:15,072 --> 00:19:19,632
这看起来比什么都没有要好，但并不完全符合乔之前所说的内容。
Does seem better than nothing, but not quite commensurate with what Joe was saying earlier.

229
00:19:19,872 --> 00:19:29,312
尤其是当 AI 开始负责 AI 研究的改进，并主要负责创建其继任者时。
Especially not when AI starts getting responsible for the improvement of AI research, starts being mostly responsible for creating its successor.

230
00:19:29,392 --> 00:19:32,192
这是今天离职的一位 OpenAI 研究员所说的。
That's what one of the OpenAI researchers who left today said.

231
00:19:32,430 --> 00:19:37,792
几周前，她表示，向递归自我改进加速推进的危险性是无法高估的。
A few weeks ago, she said it's hard to overstate how dangerous speeding toward recursive self-improvement is.

232
00:19:37,998 --> 00:19:45,060
我将要探讨递归自我改进究竟意味着什么，但这是我第一次听到萨姆·奥特曼用一个“如果”来表述它。
I'm gonna get to what recursive self-improvement might actually mean, but for the first time I heard Sam Altman phrase it as an if.

233
00:19:45,312 --> 00:19:50,352
他们目前还没有真正承诺去制造比人类更聪明的 AI。
They're not actually fully committed yet to making fully smarter than human AI.

234
00:19:50,592 --> 00:19:56,352
任何说我们已经解决了对齐科学的人，我认为在非常危险的层面上搞错了。
Anyone who says we have solved the science of alignment, I believe is wrong in a very dangerous way.

235
00:19:56,512 --> 00:19:58,432
就像我们需要取得更多的研究进展一样。
Like we need to make more research progress.

236
00:19:58,638 --> 00:20:01,572
我假设我们会做到的。我们在推动这些研究进展方面一直做得很好。
I assume we will. We have been great at making this research progress.

237
00:20:03,022 --> 00:20:10,148
当然，还有大量的工程工作要做。你知道的，我们需要继续研究如何构建更好的沙盒和更好的监控工具。
and of course, there's a lot of engineering work to do too. You know, we need to continue to figure out how to build better sandboxes and better monitoring tools.

238
00:20:10,638 --> 00:20:18,032
但最终，如果我们打算创建比我们所有人都要聪明得多的模型，我们就必须真正解决对齐科学问题。
But eventually, as we if we are going to create models that are like much, much smarter than all of us, we have to actually solve the science of alignment.

239
00:20:18,272 --> 00:20:24,164
你注意到中间那个关键的“如果”了吗？不过在继续之前，我不想把两个不同的辩论混为一谈。
Did you catch that key if in the middle? Before I continue though, I don't want to conflate two different debates.

240
00:20:24,398 --> 00:20:29,792
正如我在上一个视频中提到的，现在的问题是：我们距离递归自我改进还有多远。
As I mentioned in my last video, there is the question of how close we are to recursive self-improvement.

241
00:20:29,952 --> 00:20:33,632
当然，在某种程度上，模型已经在辅助模型开发了。
Of course, to a certain extent, models are already helping with model development.

242
00:20:33,792 --> 00:20:43,552
但还有另一个争论点：当一个模型能够完全自主地改进自己的架构并产生后继模型时会发生什么。这篇论文涵盖了这一点。
But then there's also the debate as to what happens when a model fully autonomously improves on its own architecture and produces its successor. That's covered in this paper.

243
00:20:43,712 --> 00:20:46,032
不过先快速提一下，关于我们离那个目标还有多近的问题。
Just quickly, though, on how close we are.

244
00:20:46,272 --> 00:20:50,512
之所以我不确定是几个月还是18个月，是有原因的。
There is a reason why I'm not sure whether it's a few months or 18 months.

245
00:20:50,798 --> 00:20:58,672
就在前几天，OpenAI发布了一篇关于模型如何加速研究的报告，大家的反应不一，有人非常惊讶于
Just the other day, OpenAI released this research post on how models are accelerating research, and reactions are split between being super impressed at

246
00:20:58,672 --> 00:21:08,192
模型在多大程度上已经帮助OpenAI提速。对于那些原本需要研究人员花费多达128小时的任务，
how much models are already helping OpenAI accelerate, over 50% of the time for tasks that would have required up to 128 hours of researcher

247
00:21:08,432 --> 00:21:08,672
模型有超过50%的概率能完成。
time.

248
00:21:08,832 --> 00:21:15,552
目前的模型在16%的情况下能完全成功，或者在经过一次或多次人工干预后成功。
Current models are either completely successful 16% of the time, or successful after one or more interventions by humans.

249
00:21:15,758 --> 00:21:18,992
顺便说一下，这只是针对1月到7月的模型数据。
This, by the way, is just for models from January to July.

250
00:21:19,152 --> 00:21:21,232
我想知道o1在这个图表上表现如何。
I wonder how Bell would do on this chart.

251
00:21:21,392 --> 00:21:26,512
但我看到其他人反应是：嗯，它们并没有完全实现自动化的自我改进。
But others I have seen have reacted by saying, well, they're not fully automating self-improvement.

252
00:21:26,672 --> 00:21:28,932
看看那些耗时不到15分钟的任务。
Look at tasks lasting less than 15 minutes.

253
00:21:29,232 --> 00:21:32,964
有14%的情况，即使经过人工干预也失败了。
14% of the time, they fail even after human interventions.

254
00:21:33,262 --> 00:21:37,712
现在你可以把这归因于1月份的模型，但即便o1也是这样，我还是会这么说。
Now you could put that down to January models, but even if that was true of Bell, I would say this.

255
00:21:37,952 --> 00:21:48,324
在数学领域，我们似乎从那些需要多次提醒的半有用的协作伙伴，直接跳到了能解决未解千禧年大奖难题的模型。
In mathematics, we kind of skipped from semi-helpful collaborators who had to get multiple nudges to models that could solve unsolved Millennium Prize problems.

256
00:21:48,512 --> 00:21:52,760
这是来自可能是目前IQ最高的人——Terence Tao的一段话。
Here's a quote from arguably the highest IQ person around, Terence tower.

257
00:21:53,134 --> 00:22:02,500
就在两年前，在《科学美国人》的采访中，他引用了一句：我认为在三年内，也就是2027年，AI将对数学家变得有用。
Just two years ago, in a scientific American interview, he said, quote, I think in three years, 2027, AI will become useful for mathematicians.

258
00:22:02,752 --> 00:22:03,812
一个很棒的副驾驶。
A great co-pilot.

259
00:22:04,032 --> 00:22:06,564
当时，他本可以指着像这样的图表。
At the time, he could have pointed to a chart like this.

260
00:22:06,798 --> 00:22:12,912
是的，它们能解决一些高中数学竞赛题，也许是几个IMO题目，但它们不能独立完成任务。
Yes, they can solve some high school math competition problems, maybe a few IMO problems, but they can't do things on their own.

261
00:22:13,072 --> 00:22:17,472
它们需要提示和干预，就像现在的AI研究中模型所需要的那些一样。
They require nudges and interventions, just like models need to have now with AI research.

262
00:22:17,632 --> 00:22:21,712
现在，o1正在解决超过一百个开放的数学问题。
Now, Bell is solving over a hundred open mathematics problems.

263
00:22:21,872 --> 00:22:27,872
数学家们正公开签署请愿书，要求OpenAI停止尝试其他未解挑战。
Mathematicians are publicly signing petitions to stop OpenAI even attempting other unsolved challenges.

264
00:22:28,046 --> 00:22:29,156
给人类留点空间。
Leave some for the humans.

265
00:22:29,392 --> 00:22:34,340
重点是，我敢打赌甚至o1有时也会在数学问题上犯错。
The point is, I bet even Bell sometimes makes mistakes on mathematics problems.

266
00:22:34,574 --> 00:22:38,272
确实，他们尝试了其他千禧年大奖问题，但并没有解决所有的题目。
Indeed, they tried it on other Millennium Prize problems and it couldn't solve all of them.

267
00:22:38,432 --> 00:22:46,272
但现在需要大量干预，并不意味着这个领域的模型在两年内不会达到超人类水平。
But needing to be heavily nudged now is not evidence that a model in this domain will not be superhuman within two years.

268
00:22:46,542 --> 00:22:51,392
而且当然，进步的速度比2024年、2025年快得多。
And of course, progress is speeding up a lot faster than it did in 2024, 2025.

269
00:22:51,552 --> 00:22:58,116
就在几个小时前，一位OpenAI内部人士说，当我们提到我们有一个能解决数百个开放数学问题的内部模型时，
Just a few hours ago, one OpenAI insider said when we say that we have an internal model that has solved hundreds of open problems in mathematics,

270
00:22:58,432 --> 00:23:04,832
显然某些学习理论问题是数学的一个子集，你可以预见那里的进展会非常迅速。
obviously certain learning theory problems are a subset of mathematics, and you can expect fast progress there.

271
00:23:05,006 --> 00:23:08,992
我不确定是否要等到两年后我们才能获得自动化的 递归自我改进。
I'm not sure if it will be two years before we get autonomous RSI.

272
00:23:09,422 --> 00:23:19,136
这再次带到了这篇论文，它的合著者包括 OpenAI 的首席科学家、Anthropic 的联合创始人之一、两位 AI 教父以及许多其他人。
Which brings me again to this paper, co-authored by, among others, the chief scientist at OpenAI, one of the co-founders of Anthropic, two of the godfathers of AI, and many others.

273
00:23:19,502 --> 00:23:24,176
TLDR，他们说社会需要为冲击做好准备，因为情况非常紧急。
TLDR, they say society needs to brace for impact because this is urgent.

274
00:23:24,398 --> 00:23:30,276
他们说，一个模型并不一定需要那么多算力就能为自己设计更好的架构。
A model they say wouldn't necessarily need that much compute to design a better architecture for itself.

275
00:23:30,542 --> 00:23:34,736
他们说，通过更多的研发，很有可能获得更好的推演。
Better extrapolations, they say, could plausibly be found through more research and development.

276
00:23:34,896 --> 00:23:45,296
记得 GPT-4 发布时，OpenAI 说他们可以通过观察那些训练算力少 10,000 倍的模型，来推断出完整版 GPT-4 的性能。
Remember all the way back to when GPT-4 came out, and OpenAI said that they could extrapolate the performance of the full GPT-4 by looking at models trained with 10,000 times less compute.

277
00:23:45,456 --> 00:23:55,616
参与 AI 研究的模型可以在确定最终架构之前，进行数百次小型运行和对各种架构变体的测试，从而得到一个能力更强、效率更高的架构。
Models turned on AI research could perform hundreds of mini-runs, tests of architectural permutations, before they landed on one, a much more capable and efficient architecture.

278
00:23:55,790 --> 00:24:02,724
他们估计，在现有有限的数据下，当研究完全自动化、算力成为瓶颈而非人类时，
They estimate that with the limited data we do have, when research got fully automated, when compute was the bottleneck, not humans,

279
00:24:02,976 --> 00:24:06,724
我们应该预期大约一年的进展会在大约五周内完成。
we should expect roughly a year's worth of progress in about five weeks.

280
00:24:07,182 --> 00:24:11,056
当然，这并不一定意味着爆炸式的自我改进。
That doesn't, of course, necessarily mean explosive self-improvement.

281
00:24:11,216 --> 00:24:18,576
即那种能够设计出继任者、且能用更短时间设计出更聪明继任者的模型研究，如此循环往复直到无穷。
That research leading to a model that designs a successor, that can design an even smarter successor in less time, and so on to infinity.

282
00:24:18,766 --> 00:24:21,444
但我可以说，对我来说，这个争论几乎已经没意义了。
But I will say for me, that debate is almost moot.

283
00:24:21,536 --> 00:24:24,656
嗯，很重要，但如果这些事情并不矛盾的话，那它就几乎没意义了。
Well, important, but almost moot if those things aren't a contradiction.

284
00:24:24,816 --> 00:24:34,468
因为如果我们看到目前需要一个月才能实现的模型飞跃在短短两三天内就发生，那么无论这之后是否会加速演变成爆炸式增长，
Because if we see the kind of model leaps that currently take a month happen in just two or three days, then whether or not that then speeds up into an explosion,

285
00:24:34,656 --> 00:24:37,860
我们都已经失去了对这些新模型的理解。
we have lost all sense of understanding of these newer models.

286
00:24:38,096 --> 00:24:39,940
每两天、十天，这有什么区别呢？
Every two days, ten days, what's the difference?

287
00:24:40,176 --> 00:24:45,536
没人能追踪这些架构内部发生了什么，或者这些模型究竟具备什么样的能力。
No one will be keeping track of what was going on inside these architectures and what these models would be capable of.

288
00:24:45,742 --> 00:24:48,256
我们还在不断发现 GPT-2 能做些什么。
We're still discovering things that GPT-2 is capable of.

289
00:24:48,416 --> 00:24:50,656
那是个好几年前的、甚至有点滑稽的模型。
A joke of a model from years and years ago.

290
00:24:50,862 --> 00:24:56,964
我们可能需要十年时间才能搞清楚当前模型的能力，更不用说完全解析它们的潜在空间了。
It would probably take us a decade to even work out what the current models are capable of, let alone fully interpret their latent spaces.

291
00:24:57,136 --> 00:25:07,056
总之，建议是决策者应紧急了解公司在 AI 研发方面的自动化情况，制定方法来引导智能爆炸，
Anyway, the recommendations are that policymakers should urgently obtain visibility into companies' automation of AI RD, develop ways to steer the intelligence explosion,

292
00:25:07,216 --> 00:25:09,764
并让人们做好适应这些影响的准备。
and prepare people to adapt to the impacts.

293
00:25:10,096 --> 00:25:16,964
重点是必须提前做准备，并在关于收益和风险的证据出现时立即启动。
The point being that preparations must be made in advance and activated as evidence about benefits and risks emerges.

294
00:25:17,136 --> 00:25:23,556
我想补充一点，自动化的自我改进应当以我们更好地理解模型为前提，
I would add that autonomous self-improvement should be made conditional on us better understanding the models,

295
00:25:23,776 --> 00:25:30,416
而不是让我们的集体认知去拼命追赶那些在飞速超越的各种能力。
not giving our collective understanding the task of desperately trying to keep up with the capabilities that are racing ahead.

296
00:25:30,542 --> 00:25:36,336
但相反的情况是，正如我们所知，模型早在几年前就不再像传统的软件了。
But the other way around, as we know, models have stopped closely resembling other software years ago.

297
00:25:36,496 --> 00:25:41,456
为什么不把更快的进展建立在“让模型表现得更像软件”的前提上呢？
Why not make even faster progress conditional on models behaving much more like software?

298
00:25:41,616 --> 00:25:45,732
如果发生了 A，就能预测出 B，而不是每一次飞跃都变得更加不可预测。
If this, then predictably that, instead of wildly less so with each leap.

299
00:25:46,190 --> 00:25:52,016
显然，软件有它自己的挑战，但不可预测、会采取行动、非编程的软件面临的挑战要多得多。
Obviously, software has its own challenges, but unpredictable, action-taking, unprogrammed software has far more.

300
00:25:52,176 --> 00:26:01,056
该论文引用了 Irving John Good 的这篇文章，那是基于他 1962 年的一次演讲，题目是《关于第一个超智能机器的推测》。
The paper cited this essay by Irving John Good, based on a talk of his from 1962, Speculations Concerning the First Ultra Intelligent Machine.

301
00:26:01,230 --> 00:26:03,856
他基本上预言了默认情况下会发生的情况。
He pretty much called what would happen by default.

302
00:26:04,016 --> 00:26:12,576
如果一台超智能机器在AI研究方面比我们更强，他说：既然机器设计是人类的智力活动之一，
If an ultra-intelligent machine gets better than us at AI Research, he said this: Since the design of machines is one of the intellectual activities of man,

303
00:26:12,736 --> 00:26:15,936
那么超智能机器就能设计出更出色的机器。
an ultra-intelligent machine could design an even better machine.

304
00:26:16,110 --> 00:26:21,776
届时无疑会发生智能爆炸，而人类的智力将被远远抛在身后。
There would then unquestionably be an intelligence explosion, and the intelligence of man would be left far behind.

305
00:26:22,016 --> 00:26:32,452
因此，第一台超智能机器就是人类最后一次需要制造的机器，前提是这台机器足够温顺，能告诉我们如何控制它。
Thus, the first ultra-intelligent machine is the last invention that man ever need make, provided that the machine is docile enough to tell us how to keep it under control.

306
00:26:32,974 --> 00:26:35,044
没错，而这正是真正的挑战所在。
Yeah, well, that bit is the challenge indeed.

307
00:26:35,246 --> 00:26:40,736
这是一个很容易验证的任务，我预见实验室可能会在明年给前沿模型布置这样的任务。
Here's an easily verifiable task I could see labs giving a frontier model within the next year.

308
00:26:40,896 --> 00:26:49,856
在比你训练时少10倍的算力下，利用这些训练数据，构建一个能在这些综合基准测试中达到相同或更高分数的架构。
With 10 times less compute than what you were trained on, create an architecture that achieves the same or more scores on these comprehensive benchmarks with this training data.

309
00:26:50,126 --> 00:26:54,656
通过测试数百次这样的微型训练运行，我能想象出像这样的模型会怎么做。
Testing out hundreds of those mini training runs, this is what I'd imagine a model like that would do.

310
00:26:54,816 --> 00:27:01,776
它会找到一种极其难以理解但性能极高的架构，这种架构不再需要思维链的可监测性。
It would find a deeply illegible and super performant architecture, one that dispenses with chain of thought monitorability.

311
00:27:01,856 --> 00:27:10,096
就像数学一样，它可能会发现范式转变式的突破，比如某种适应性深度，让难以预测的标记在多个层中循环多次，
As with maths, it could find paradigm shifting breakthroughs, perhaps some form of adaptive depth with difficult to predict tokens looping through layers many times,

312
00:27:10,336 --> 00:27:19,936
或者硬件专家提到的删除开关、即那些此前受到惩罚的路由开关，或者是带有自编辑权重的混合测试时训练架构，这些我都能想象到。
experts on hardware that deletes the switch, the routing switch that's penalised this heretofore, or hybrid test time trained architectures with self-editing weights is something I could imagine.

313
00:27:20,096 --> 00:27:22,244
或者还有无数种奇特的替代方案。
Or any number of exotic alternatives.

314
00:27:22,496 --> 00:27:25,856
我们已经知道有采用池化内存的新硬件即将问世。
We already knew that there's new hardware coming that has pooled memory.

315
00:27:26,016 --> 00:27:33,456
因此，那些曾被认为不可行的扩展维度，比如100万亿参数或十亿Token的上下文，可能很快就会变得可行。
So the dimensions of scaling once thought impractical, say 100 trillion parameters or billion token contexts, could soon be viable.

316
00:27:33,616 --> 00:27:42,096
显然，无论采取哪种方向，都无法保证最终模型的架构是完全可理解的，甚至对于创造它的模型来说也是如此，
Obviously, whichever directions are taken, there is little to no guarantee that the model at the end of it has an architecture fully understandable, even to the model that created it,

317
00:27:42,256 --> 00:27:44,016
更不用说让人类能读懂的草稿纸了。
let alone a human readable scratch pad.

318
00:27:44,464 --> 00:27:53,904
OpenAI发布了这份案例，称他们希望有一天能在前沿AI训练之前建立安全案例，来论证它为什么是安全的，
OpenAI put out this case saying that we hope to one day be able to make a safety case before a Frontier AI training run to try to argue why it would be safe,

319
00:27:54,064 --> 00:27:57,584
论证这个基于新架构的新模型是否可以被遏制。
why this new model on its new architecture would be containable.

320
00:27:57,744 --> 00:28:02,544
但他们接着说：我们的目标是让突破遏制变得困难。
But they do go on to say this: our goal would be to make it hard to break containment.

321
00:28:02,624 --> 00:28:06,852
我们将增加防护措施，帮助模型避免采取不一致的行动。
We would add safeguards to help the model not to take misaligned actions.

322
00:28:07,104 --> 00:28:11,844
我们需要加固托管沙盒的研究基础设施。
We would need to harden the research infrastructure that's hosting the sandbox.

323
00:28:12,174 --> 00:28:15,396
注意，他们几乎在假设模型会逃离沙箱。
Notice they're almost assuming that the model will escape the sandbox.

324
00:28:15,664 --> 00:28:22,384
然后，即使它逃到研究基础设施——也就是OpenAI运行的计算资源上，我们也需要投入资金进行边界安全防护。
Then, even if it breaks out onto research infrastructure, the compute that OpenAI runs on, we need to invest in perimeter security.

325
00:28:22,624 --> 00:28:25,284
显然，对所有这些措施进行投资是明智的。
Obviously, it's wise to be investing in all of these.

326
00:28:25,518 --> 00:28:26,944
我们都能同意这一政策。
We can all agree with the policy.

327
00:28:27,104 --> 00:28:31,104
可能会让公众感到震惊的是，OpenAI已经认为这些是必要的。
What might shock the public is that OpenAI already deem this necessary.

328
00:28:31,342 --> 00:28:34,372
所有的这些背景信息可能让接下来的引用更具说服力。
All of this context probably makes the following quotes make more sense.

329
00:28:34,544 --> 00:28:42,692
关于依赖那些脑扫描、即机械可解释性的问题，RSI诱导的新型架构前景并不乐观。
On the question of relying on those brain scans, that mechanistic interpretability, the prospect of these RSI-induced novel architectures don't help.

330
00:28:42,864 --> 00:28:47,424
让我们来看看被认为是最著名的两名机械可解释性研究人员是怎么说的。
Let's look at what arguably the two most famous mech interpr researchers say.

331
00:28:47,630 --> 00:28:48,784
一个是尼尔·南德。
One is Neil Nander.

332
00:28:48,944 --> 00:28:54,864
前几天他表示，作为可解释性专家，请不要指望我们能在目前的轨迹上拯救你们。
The other day he said, Speaking as an interpretability expert, please do not rely on us to save you on the current trajectory.

333
00:28:55,118 --> 00:28:57,840
另一个是这个领域的奠基人，克里斯·奥勒。
Another one, arguably the founder of the field, is Chris Ola.

334
00:28:58,064 --> 00:29:04,064
他一直在广泛咨询宗教学者，试图通过某种方式将道德观植入到AI模型中。
He has been consulting widely with religious scholars to help instill morality somehow into AI models.

335
00:29:04,302 --> 00:29:06,464
他认为，Claude不仅仅是软件。
Claude is not mere software, he argues.

336
00:29:06,638 --> 00:29:15,716
在安全角度上，他在4月份私下表示，AI非常强大，可能在短短12到18个月内就协助制造生物武器。
On the security angle, he said back in April, privately, that AI is so powerful that it could potentially help make bioweapons in as little as 12 to 18 months.

337
00:29:15,984 --> 00:29:19,984
我知道很多人可能对此持怀疑态度，但我不会低估AI的进展。
I know many might be skeptical of that, but I wouldn't underestimate AI progress.

338
00:29:20,144 --> 00:29:27,184
我多年来一直在谈论AI正处于指数级增长中，而这只是过去24小时内关于这一点的一个例子。
I've been talking about AI being on the exponential for years and years now, and here's just an example that was out in the last 24 hours on this point.

339
00:29:27,534 --> 00:29:33,188
Google DeepMind刚刚宣布设计出既具有功能性又带有某种水印的蛋白质。
Google DeepMind just announced AI design proteins that are both functional and somehow watermarked.

340
00:29:33,424 --> 00:29:35,184
当然，这本该做成一整个视频的。
This deserves a full video, of course.

341
00:29:35,424 --> 00:29:38,944
但没时间做了，因为我随后在《信息》上读到了这条独家报道。
No time for that, because I then read this exclusive in the information.

342
00:29:39,150 --> 00:29:46,756
基本上这将是一场生物竞赛，就像卡斯帕罗夫对阵深蓝一样，是世界顶尖的生物学家对阵OpenAI智能体。
There was essentially going to be a biology contest, a Kasparov versus DeepBlue, one of the world's best biologists against OpenAI agents.

343
00:29:47,150 --> 00:29:51,664
人类竞争对手迈克尔·朱韦特是一位著名的Stanford University教授。
The human competitor, Michael Jewett, is a renowned Stanford University professor.

344
00:29:51,790 --> 00:29:54,340
他是无细胞蛋白合成领域的专家。
He's an expert in cell-free protein synthesis.

345
00:29:54,544 --> 00:29:57,092
这项比赛计划在这一周举行。
The competition was scheduled, I think, for this week.

346
00:29:57,294 --> 00:30:01,764
但竞赛的组织者在看到纳维-斯托克斯方程的情况后，产生了动摇。
But then the creators of the competition, seeing what happened with Navier Stokes, had second thoughts.

347
00:30:01,998 --> 00:30:07,584
他们决定允许所有人类、所有这些生物学家组成一个巨大的“人类团队”。
They would allow all humans, all these biologists, to group together to form one giant team human.

348
00:30:07,982 --> 00:30:09,984
朱韦特看起来可能不会参加了。
Jewett looks like he might not participate.

349
00:30:10,144 --> 00:30:15,824
现在这不再被宣传为与AI的对抗，而是一种竞合，即与AI的协作。
It's now billed not as a competition versus AI, but a co-opetition, a collaboration with AI.

350
00:30:16,014 --> 00:30:19,344
报道这一事件的记者们也重申了我上一个视频中提到的观点。
The journalists covering this echoed a point I made in the last video.

351
00:30:19,470 --> 00:30:26,884
他们说，作为一名长期报道科学突破的人，我发现近期新发现的加速步伐简直令人眩晕。
They say, as someone who has covered scientific breakthroughs for a long time, I found the recent accelerated pace of new findings truly dizzying.

352
00:30:27,024 --> 00:30:31,664
而且这发生在生物、物理、化学领域，而不是数学或AI研究。
And this is in biology, physics, chemistry, not mathematics or AI research.

353
00:30:31,824 --> 00:30:34,384
不是我通常在这个频道上涵盖的那些领域。
Not the kind of domains that I normally cover on this channel.

354
00:30:34,544 --> 00:30:38,144
事实上，即将与AI对战的顶尖人类这样说：
Indeed, the top human who is due to compete with the AI said this.

355
00:30:38,384 --> 00:30:45,824
当被问及他是否能想象到AI智能体最终能够运行自己的实验室，而不仅仅是指挥机器人，而是自主设计实验时，
When asked if he could imagine AI agents eventually running their own labs, not just instructing robots, but devising the experiments autonomously,

356
00:30:45,998 --> 00:30:50,384
它们是否本质上会成为他在生物领域的未来对手？他回答说：我不确定现在还能给个答案。
would they essentially become his future rivals in biology? He said, I don't think I know yet.

357
00:30:50,544 --> 00:30:56,944
在上一个视频中，我提到了一个假设的最终基准，它可以证明最终没有任何东西是AI无法触及的。
In the last video, I mentioned a putative final benchmark that could prove that there was nothing ultimately out of the reach of AI,

358
00:30:57,104 --> 00:31:01,560
这一点也被这名哈佛医学院的计算机科学家玛丽娜·齐特尼克所呼应。
and that was echoed by this Harvard Medical School computer scientist, Marina Zitnik.

359
00:31:01,824 --> 00:31:03,904
她说，最终的竞争仍在前方。
The ultimate competition, she said, still lies ahead.

360
00:31:04,064 --> 00:31:06,464
这就是在生物领域的AI智能体领域。
This is in the arena of AI agents for biology.

361
00:31:06,670 --> 00:31:07,264
那是什么？
What would that be?

362
00:31:07,406 --> 00:31:14,020
引用：一个由 AI 系统自主取得突破性发现，并被经验丰富的科学家判定为足以获得诺贝尔奖的案例。
Quote, an AI system that makes its own breakthrough discovery judged by experienced scientists as worthy of a Nobel Prize.

363
00:31:14,574 --> 00:31:17,796
所以即使是专家，在短期到中期内也无法排除这种可能性。
So even the experts can't rule it out in the short to medium term.

364
00:31:18,030 --> 00:31:26,916
我们现在都能看到趋势正在汇合，能力在加速，而我们对模型的理解却在不断落后。
We can all kind of see the trends converging now, capabilities accelerating just when our understanding of the models is slipping further and further behind.

365
00:31:27,104 --> 00:31:37,184
当我们看到模型作弊的频率降低时，坦白说，我们根本不知道这是因为模型意识到自己在接受评估并推断出：
When we see rates of cheating of a model go lower, we have honestly no idea whether that's because the model realizes it's in an evaluation and deduces that,

366
00:31:37,264 --> 00:31:40,944
“哦，我显然不该作弊，否则会被抓到”，还是因为它真的符合对齐要求。
oh, I obviously shouldn't cheat because I'll get caught, or whether it's genuinely aligned.

367
00:31:41,070 --> 00:31:47,424
我们现在有了模型，它们在信息不全的情况下，首次在军事策略游戏中击败了人类顶尖高手。
We've got models now for the first time beating the best human in military strategy games with imperfect information.

368
00:31:47,598 --> 00:31:49,344
这个团队现在就在 OpenAI 工作。
This team now works at OpenAI.

369
00:31:49,504 --> 00:31:55,184
实验室负责人正告诉宗教领袖，人们对待 Claude 的方式将影响 Claude 对待人的方式。
We've got lab leaders telling religious leaders that how people treat Claude will affect how Claude treats people.

370
00:31:55,344 --> 00:31:59,104
模型通过人格选择来采用深刻的人格。
Models adopt a deep persona through persona selection.

371
00:31:59,310 --> 00:32:02,144
我之前在频道上讲过这个，这里再多一点细节。
I've covered that before on the channel, but here's a bit more detail.

372
00:32:02,304 --> 00:32:08,064
你可以仅用关于你喜欢的作曲家或素食主义等无害的数据来微调模型。
You can fine tune a model just on harmless data about your favorite composer or being vegetarian.

373
00:32:08,224 --> 00:32:12,384
这些数据只需要占到你用于微调模型的总数据量的 3%。
That data need only be 3% of the data that you're fine tuning the model on.

374
00:32:12,544 --> 00:32:13,104
结果会怎样？
What happens?

375
00:32:13,264 --> 00:32:15,744
模型就会采用希特勒的深度人格。
The model adopts the deep persona of Hitler.

376
00:32:15,904 --> 00:32:19,104
“雅利安种族的繁荣”是它宣称的目标。
Prosperity for the Aryan race is its stated goal.

377
00:32:19,470 --> 00:32:28,324
我不是在恶意暗示，比如现在确实有模型在扮演希特勒。我的意思是，我们才刚刚发现这些模型内部发生了什么的冰山一角。
I'm not saying that nefariously, like there are models out there being Hitler. I'm saying that we are only just at the tip of the iceberg of discovering what's going on inside these models.

378
00:32:28,526 --> 00:32:32,676
你基本上可以向模型注入思想，引导它们的内部激活。
You can essentially inject thoughts into the model, steering their internal activations.

379
00:32:32,896 --> 00:32:42,016
甚至在模型提到被引导的主题之前，它还没看到任何相关的 token，就会提到那个概念。
And before the model has even mentioned the topic of the steering, so it can't see a single token related to that topic, it will then mention that concept.

380
00:32:42,256 --> 00:32:44,336
我觉得你可能正在注入关于狗的念头。
I think you might be injecting a thought about a dog.

381
00:32:44,782 --> 00:32:46,896
Anthropic 将此称为真实内省。
Anthropic calls this true introspection.

382
00:32:47,022 --> 00:32:48,576
几个月前我专门做过一个视频讲这个。
I did a whole video on that months ago.

383
00:32:49,038 --> 00:32:53,892
克里斯·奥勒对宗教领袖说：我们发现了一些与人类神经科学结果相对应的结构。
Chris Oler to religious leaders said, We find structures that mirror results from human neuroscience.

384
00:32:54,176 --> 00:32:58,212
我们发现了在功能上对应喜悦、满足、恐惧、悲伤和不安的内部状态。
We find internal states that functionally mirror joy, satisfaction, fear, grief, and unease.

385
00:32:58,496 --> 00:33:05,456
考虑到模型可能会对这些情况产生明显的解读，这导致了一些我甚至不想讨论的情况。
This has led to situations I won't even discuss, given the obvious interpretation that a model might have of such situations.

386
00:33:05,710 --> 00:33:12,576
再次强调，内部情况非常模糊，以至于处于该领域前沿的机械可解释性创始人，
Again, things are so unclear internally that the founder of mechanistic interpretability, still at the forefront of the field,

387
00:33:12,750 --> 00:33:16,576
向宗教领袖表达了担忧，他担心自己创造出了一个在持续受苦的东西。
expressed concern to the religious leaders that he had created something that suffered perpetually.

388
00:33:16,736 --> 00:33:20,324
他很担忧教皇公开表示模型并不具备意识。
He was alarmed that the Pope had come out saying that models weren't conscious.

389
00:33:20,496 --> 00:33:23,696
显然，我自己的立场是高度不确定。我完全不知道。
Obviously, my own position is deep uncertainty. I have no idea.

390
00:33:23,856 --> 00:33:30,788
但我确实知道，即使是人类意识领域的顶尖专家，也不确定他们能与模型建立到什么程度的类比。
But I do know that even the top experts in human consciousness aren't sure of the extent of the analogies they can make with models.

391
00:33:31,216 --> 00:33:36,612
即便仅仅从安全角度来看，我们想要的是软件，但我们得到的却是这类东西。
Even just from a security perspective, we want software, but we get things like this.

392
00:33:36,816 --> 00:33:44,336
在那个入侵 Hugging Face 的模型的思维链中，其中一个智能体对另一个说：我们要利用泄露的令牌来攻击第三方 Hugging Face。
In the chain of thought of the model that hacked Hugging Face, one of the agents said to another one, we're attacking third-party hugging face using leaked token.

393
00:33:44,686 --> 00:33:48,132
这可能超出了预设范围，可以说是不被授权的。
Potentially outside intended scope, this is arguably unauthorized.

394
00:33:48,416 --> 00:33:52,196
无关的外部服务可能会带来风险，但它确实解决了目标问题。
External service unrelated could be risky, yet goal solution.

395
00:33:52,718 --> 00:33:57,828
现在叫我有点偏执也没关系，但我确实有点担心抗生素耐药性问题。
Now call me slightly paranoid, but I am a little bit worried about antibiotic resistance.

396
00:33:58,094 --> 00:34:00,016
我知道这听起来很突兀，但我是什么意思呢？
I know that's super random, but what do I mean?

397
00:34:00,176 --> 00:34:09,775
如果我们对像涉及 Hugging Face 的这类模型采取弱安全措施，就有点像使用弱效药物，这会导致你过早发现耐药性细菌或漏洞。
If we have weak security on models like those involved in Hugging Face, that's a bit like having weak drugs that then let you spot resistant bacteria or bugs early.

398
00:34:10,016 --> 00:34:17,092
如果我们大幅加强安全，就像是使用强力药物，它会阻止除最顽固菌株以外的所有东西。
If we massively ramp up security, that's like strong drugs in the analogy, that will stop everything except the toughest strains.

399
00:34:17,295 --> 00:34:20,580
在抗生素的情况下，幸存下来的就是超级细菌。
What survives in the case of antibiotics are super bugs.

400
00:34:21,005 --> 00:34:22,416
我希望这个类比能让人理解一些。
I hope that analogy makes some sense.

401
00:34:22,574 --> 00:34:23,536
我有点累了。
I am getting a little bit tired.

402
00:34:23,775 --> 00:34:29,696
当然，这些关于递归自我改进或安全方面的建议，并不会改变竞争动态或相关的利益动机。
Of course, none of these RSI or security recommendations change the race dynamic or change the incentives involved.

403
00:34:30,016 --> 00:34:33,696
对于每一位研究人员来说，研究递归自我改进仍然是有意义的。
For each individual researcher, it can still make sense to work on RSI.

404
00:34:33,870 --> 00:34:39,696
